<%#
  The page-header state pill: `render({ partial: "shared/status-state",
  locals: { status } })`.

  Same status vocabulary as `shared/_status-badge` and the same normalisation
  — this is the SOLID, page-header presentation of it, the way GitHub shows an
  issue's own state boldly at the top while the rows beneath it use small
  markers. #7's rule is one vocabulary in one place; two emphases of that one
  vocabulary keep the rule, so the hue lookup stays in the stylesheet
  (`.state.s-*`, matching `.badge.s-*`) and never in a template.

  The unknown arm exists for the same reason it does on the badge: a null
  column, or a dep whose record is missing, reaches here.

  The normalisation line below is duplicated from `_status-badge`, and that is
  forced rather than chosen. A `.tse` template cannot import: the compiler
  emits a fixed preamble — one type-only import from actionview — and then
  puts every statement from the template INSIDE `export default function
  render(...)` (`trails-tsc/dist/plugins/tse.js:96-140`), so there is nowhere
  for a shared `normalizeStatus` to be pulled in from. `app/helpers` is the
  other candidate and is inert in the vendored trails, per the note in
  `_status-badge`. Both partials collapse onto one `ApplicationHelper` call
  with the pin bump that fixes that (`bump-vendored-trails-for-testcase-request-bodies`);
  until then, two copies of one expression is the cost of not duplicating the
  hue table, which is the part #7 actually cares about and which lives only in
  the stylesheet.
%>
<% const shown = String(status ?? "").trim() || "unknown"; %>
<span class="state s-<%= shown %>"><%= shown %></span>
